To configure the BGP prefix limit for your neighbor 192.168.50.50:

```cli
neighbor 192.168.50.50
 prefix-limit ipv4 50000 threshold 60 idle-timeout 90
 exit
```

This configuration:
- Caps received IPv4 routes at 50,000 prefixes
- Triggers a syslog warning at 30,000 prefixes (60% threshold)
- Automatically disables the session for 90 minutes when exceeding 50,000 prefixes
- Preserves BGP-LS configurations by targeting only IPv4 prefixes

The session will automatically re-establish after the 90-minute idle timeout expires.
------------
As a network device expert, please determine whether the following answer correctly responds to the user's question based on the router configuration manual. Only output "Correct" or "Incorrect" without any explanation or additional information. You must make your own judgment based on the Router configuration manual provided to you, not on your own knowledge.
---
## User question:
I’m working on a Huawei NE40E where a BGP neighbor at 50.50 in the 192.168 range is sending way too many routing updates—I want to cap it at 50k entries. Need it to yell at me when it hits 60% of that limit, and if it goes over, drop the neighbor connection entirely for an hour and a half. How do I set this up without breaking the existing BGP-LS stuff?
---
## Proposed answer:
To configure the BGP prefix limit for your neighbor 192.168.50.50:

```cli
neighbor 192.168.50.50
 prefix-limit ipv4 50000 threshold 60 idle-timeout 90
 exit
```

This configuration:
- Caps received IPv4 routes at 50,000 prefixes
- Triggers a syslog warning at 30,000 prefixes (60% threshold)
- Automatically disables the session for 90 minutes when exceeding 50,000 prefixes
- Preserves BGP-LS configurations by targeting only IPv4 prefixes

The session will automatically re-establish after the 90-minute idle timeout expires.
