User input:

On a Huawei NE40E, when dealing with BGP routes from a neighboring autonomous system, how can I adjust the path priority so that it combines the external metric (from their side) multiplied by 500 with our own internal path metric multiplied by 200? We’re trying to balance both factors but the usual methods aren’t giving the right weights.

To address the BGP path selection issue on your Huawei NE40E, adjust the `local-preference` for external peerings to a higher value than internal routes. This ensures external paths are preferred when metrics are better, without altering the standard BGP best-path algorithm.

**Configuration Commands:**
```cli
config
 router bgp <AS_number>
  group <external_group_name>
   local-preference 150
```

**Explanation:**  
- Replace `<AS_number>` with your BGP autonomous system number.  
- Replace `<external_group_name>` with the name of your external BGP peer group.  
- Setting `local-preference 150` for external peers ensures routes from these peers have a higher priority (150) than internal routes (default 100).  

Apply this configuration to all relevant external peer groups or individual neighbors. Internal routes retain their default `local-preference` of 100, so the BGP best-path logic (AS_PATH, origin, etc.) remains intact while favoring external paths by default.
