To temporarily disable the BGP session to the neighbor at 192.168.5.5 without removing its configuration, use the `shutdown` command under the specific neighbor context. Here are the CLI commands:

```
configure terminal
router bgp
group <group-name>
neighbor 192.168.5.5
shutdown
exit
exit
exit
```

Replace `<group-name>` with the actual BGP group name where the neighbor is configured. This administratively disables the session while preserving all settings. To restore the session later, repeat the steps but use `no shutdown` instead. 

**Note:** The exact group name must be specified, as BGP neighbors are configured within group contexts. If unsure, check the current configuration with `display current-configuration | include 192.168.5.5` to identify the group name first.
