## User question:
Working on a Huawei NE40E with multiple VPN instances over BGP for IPv4—when I pull routes from one instance into another and share them with internal BGP neighbors, the next hop addresses keep getting swapped to local interfaces. How do I stop that from happening so the neighbors see the original gateway info from the source routes?
---
## Proposed answer:
To address the issue of next-hop addresses being swapped to local interfaces when sharing routes between VPN instances and advertising to internal BGP neighbors, configure the following:

```cli
bgp
  group <INTERNAL_GROUP_NAME>
    next-hop-unchanged
```

Replace `<INTERNAL_GROUP_NAME>` with the name of your BGP group for internal neighbors. This command ensures the original next-hop information from the source routes is preserved when advertising to peers in the specified group.

If `next-hop-self` is currently enabled for the group or specific neighbors, disable it to prevent conflicts:
```cli
bgp
  group <INTERNAL_GROUP_NAME>
    no next-hop-self
    next-hop-unchanged
```

For neighbor-specific configuration (if not using groups):
```cli
bgp
  group <GROUP_NAME>
    neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP>
      no next-hop-self
      next-hop-unchanged
```

These commands disable next-hop modification and maintain the original gateway information from the source VPN instance routes.
