To establish external BGP neighbors for "GROUP_A" under the virtual routing table "VPN_B" when the neighbors are not on the same subnet and are multiple hops away, use the following CLI configuration commands:

```bash
bgp
 group GROUP_A external
 peer GROUP_A ebgp-max-hop 10
 ipv4-family vpn-instance VPN_B
  peer GROUP_A enable
 exit
 neighbor <neighbor-ip>
  peer <neighbor-ip> group GROUP_A
  peer <neighbor-ip> vpn-instance VPN_B
```

**Explanation:**
- `group GROUP_A external`: Creates an external BGP group named "GROUP_A".
- `peer GROUP_A ebgp-max-hop 10`: Configures eBGP multihop with a TTL of 10 to allow peering across 10 network hops.
- `ipv4-family vpn-instance VPN_B`: Enters the VPNv4 address family context for the virtual routing table "VPN_B".
- `peer GROUP_A enable`: Activates the VPNv4 address family for "GROUP_A" peers.
- `neighbor <neighbor-ip>`: Specifies the IP address of the BGP neighbor.
- `peer <neighbor-ip> group GROUP_A`: Assigns the neighbor to "GROUP_A".
- `peer <neighbor-ip> vpn-instance VPN_B`: Binds the neighbor to the virtual routing table "VPN_B".

Replace `<neighbor-ip>` with the actual IP address of your BGP neighbor. This configuration enables eBGP multihop for the group and associates the neighbor with the specified VPN instance.
