## Proposed answer:
Based on your request to overwrite the original network identifier in the AS path with your own AS when advertising routes to BGP neighbor 192.168.1.2, use the following configuration:

```cli
system-view
bgp <your_as_number>
 group <group_name>
  neighbor 192.168.1.2
   remove-private
  exit
 exit
exit
commit
```

Replace `<your_as_number>` with your BGP autonomous system number and `<group_name>` with your BGP peer group name. The `remove-private` command strips private AS numbers from the AS_PATH attribute before advertising routes to the specified neighbor, substituting them with your network's AS identifier. This ensures the legacy AS information is masked during route advertisement.
