To adjust the TCP maximum segment size (MSS) for BGP sessions with the neighbor at 192.168.50.45 to 1500 bytes, use the following commands:

```
config
 router bgp
  group <your-group-name>
   neighbor 192.168.50.45
    tcp mss 1500
```

Replace `<your-group-name>` with the appropriate BGP group name. This configuration sets the MSS for the specified neighbor to avoid fragmentation during the BGP session establishment.
------------
As a network device expert, please determine whether the following answer correctly responds to the user's question based on the router configuration manual. Only output "Correct" or "Incorrect" without any explanation or additional information. You must make your own judgment based on the Router configuration manual provided to you, not on your own knowledge.
---
## User question:
Hey, I'm working on a Huawei NE40E and need to set up BGP sessions with a neighbor at 192.168.50.45, but our path seems to have MTU constraints. How do I adjust the biggest TCP data chunk allowed during handshake for these sessions to 1500 bytes? Trying to avoid fragmentation issues when establishing the connection.
---
## Proposed answer:
To adjust the TCP maximum segment size (MSS) for BGP sessions with the neighbor at 192.168.50.45 to 1500 bytes, use the following commands:

```
config
 router bgp
  group <your-group-name>
   neighbor 192.168.50.45
    tcp mss 1500
```

Replace `<your-group-name>` with the appropriate BGP group name. This configuration sets the MSS for the specified neighbor to avoid fragmentation during the BGP session establishment.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
peer <ipv4-address> tcp-mss <tcp-mss-number>
undo peer <ipv4-address> tcp-mss
FuncDef:
The peer tcp-mss command configures a TCP MSS value used when the local device establishes TCP connections with a peer.
The undo peer tcp-mss command deletes the configured TCP MSS value.
By default, no TCP MSS is configured for the local device to establish TCP connections with a peer.
ParaDef:
{'Parameters': 'ipv4-address', 'Info': 'Specifies the IPv4 address of a peer.\nThe value is in dotted decimal notation.'}
{'Parameters': 'tcp-mss-number', 'Info': 'Specifies the TCP MSS value used for TCP connection establishment.\nThe value is an integer ranging from 176 to 4096, in bytes.'}
