To address the issue of stripping unnecessary route tags from BGP updates received from the specific peer at 192.168.5.2, use the following CLI commands. These commands will remove all types of communities (standard, extended, and large) from incoming routes without impacting core path attributes like AS_PATH or next-hop:

```
configure router bgp
  group <group-name>
    neighbor 192.168.5.2
      disable-communities standard extended large
```

**Notes:**  
1. Replace `<group-name>` with the BGP group name where the neighbor 192.168.5.2 is configured.  
2. The `disable-communities` command explicitly strips community metadata while preserving essential routing information.  
3. This configuration applies only to the specified neighbor (192.168.5.2), leaving other peers unaffected.  

