As a network device expert, please determine whether the following answer correctly responds to the user's question based on the router configuration manual. Only output "Correct" or "Incorrect" without any explanation or additional information. You must make your own judgment based on the Router configuration manual provided to you, not on your own knowledge.
---
## User question:
Hey, I’m working with a Huawei NE40E where our VPNv4 setup is accidentally leaking some big labeled BGP supernet routes. How do I tweak the config to block these from being advertised in the IPv4 VPN instance?
---
## Proposed answer:
To prevent the leakage of labeled BGP supernet routes in your IPv4 VPN instance, configure the following:

```cli
config>router>bgp
    best-path-selection
        no as-path-ignore vpn-ipv4
```

This command ensures AS path length is considered during BGP best-path selection for VPN-IPv4 routes, preventing supernet routes from being prioritized and advertised incorrectly.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
supernet unicast advertise enable
supernet unicast advertise disable
supernet label-route advertise enable
supernet label-route advertise disable
undo supernet unicast advertise enable
undo supernet label-route advertise disable
FuncDef:
The supernet unicast advertise enable command configures a BGP device to advertise BGP supernet unicast routes to its peers.
The undo supernet unicast advertise enable or supernet unicast advertise disable command restores the default configuration.
The supernet label-route advertise disable command disables a BGP device from advertising labeled BGP supernet routes.
The undo supernet label-route advertise disable or supernet label-route advertise enable command restores the default configuration.
By default, labeled BGP supernet routes can be preferentially selected and advertised. BGP supernet unicast routes are considered invalid and cannot be advertised to BGP peers or delivered to the IP routing table.
