To configure BGP neighbors with legacy equipment that doesn't support AS federation, use the following commands. Replace placeholders (`<PEER_IP>`, `<OLD_PEER_AS>`, `<VIRTUAL_LOCAL_AS>`, `<LOCAL_IP>`) with your actual values:

```cli
router bgp <GLOBAL_AS>
 group LEGACY_GROUP
  peer-as <OLD_PEER_AS>
  local-as <VIRTUAL_LOCAL_AS> private no-prepend-global-as
  local-address <LOCAL_IP>
  neighbor <PEER_IP>
 exit
```

**Explanation:**
- `peer-as <OLD_PEER_AS>`: Sets the legacy peer's actual AS number.
- `local-as <VIRTUAL_LOCAL_AS>`: Advertises a virtual local AS to the legacy peer instead of your global AS.
- `private no-prepend-global-as`: Hides both the virtual AS from internal path propagation and avoids prepending your global AS in updates to the legacy peer.
- `local-address <LOCAL_IP>`: Specifies the source IP for the BGP session.

**Example with sample values:**
```cli
router bgp 65000
 group LEGACY_GROUP
  peer-as 64512
  local-as 64515 private no-prepend-global-as
  local-address 10.0.0.1
  neighbor 10.0.0.2
 exit
``` 

This configuration allows the legacy device to peer using the virtual AS (`64515`) while maintaining your actual global AS (`65000`) internally. The legacy device will only see the virtual AS in the AS_PATH.
------------
