## User question:
Hey, on our Huawei NE40E, I'm trying to make sure those extra-large BGP group tags get shared with our "internet_peers" crew when exchanging routes—how do I tweak the settings so they actually show up for those neighbors?
---
## Proposed answer:
To ensure the extra-large BGP group tags (link-bandwidth extended community) are shared with your "internet_peers" group during route exchanges, apply these CLI configuration commands:

```
config
 router bgp
  group internet_peers
   link-bandwidth
    send-to-ebgp ipv4
   exit
  exit
 exit
```

**Explanation:**  
- The `link-bandwidth` command enters the configuration context for handling extended communities under the BGP group.  
- `send-to-ebgp ipv4` explicitly enables advertising the link-bandwidth extended community to all EBGP peers in the "internet_peers" group for IPv4 unicast routes.  

This configuration ensures the extended community attributes are included when exchanging routes with the specified peer group.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
peer <group-name> advertise-large-community
undo peer <group-name> advertise-large-community
FuncDef:
The peer advertise-large-community command enables a device to advertise the Large-Community attribute to a BGP peer group.
The undo peer advertise-large-community command cancels the configuration.
By default, a device does not advertise the Large-Community attribute to its BGP peer group.
ParaDef:
{'Parameters': 'group-name', 'Info': 'Specifies the name of a peer group.\nThe name is a string of 1 to 47 case-sensitive characters, with spaces not supported. When double quotation marks are used around the string, spaces are allowed in the string.'}
