## User question:
On a Huawei NE40E box, when pushing route updates to our internal BGP neighbor group called "internal_peers" within the same AS, how do we make the device always swap the next-hop attribute to its own loopback or interface address instead of preserving what it received from external peers? Trying to avoid path confusion in the core.
---
## Proposed answer:
To configure the device to always set the next-hop attribute to its own address for the BGP group "internal_peers", use the following commands:

```bash
system-view
bgp
group internal_peers
next-hop-self
```

This ensures routes advertised to the "internal_peers" group use the local router's address (e.g., loopback or interface) as the next-hop, overriding any external next-hop values.
