To ensure the router uses its own traffic-handling priority levels for the neighbor 192.168.5.6 instead of BGP-assigned path markers, configure a specific route preference (administrative distance) for routes learned from this peer. Replace `<group-name>` with the actual BGP group name where the neighbor is configured.

```cli
config router bgp
  group <group-name>
    neighbor 192.168.5.6
      preference 100
    exit
  exit
exit
```

**Explanation:**  
The `preference` command overrides the default BGP route priority (170) for routes received from 192.168.5.6. Setting it to `100` (or your desired priority value between 1–255) ensures the router prioritizes these routes using your locally defined rules instead of the peer's BGP path attributes.
