## User question:
On Huawei NE40E boxes, when sharing routes with our internal BGP buddy list called "internal_peers," how do I force the router to point those routes to itself as the next stop instead of passing along whatever hop info it received? Trying to fix traffic blackholing because neighbors can’t reach the original next-hop IPs.
---
## Proposed answer:
To address the traffic blackholing issue by forcing BGP routes shared with your "internal_peers" group to use the local router as the next-hop, apply the following configuration:

```cli
configure router bgp
 group internal_peers
  next-hop-self
 exit
exit
```

**Explanation:**  
The `next-hop-self` command ensures that when routes are advertised to peers in the "internal_peers" group, the next-hop IP is set to a local address of this router (instead of propagating the original next-hop). This resolves reachability problems if peers cannot access the original next-hop IPs. The configuration is applied at the BGP group level for efficiency.
