## User question:
On a Huawei NE40E, if I want routes from my BGP neighbor crew "group_alpha" to be prioritized with a magic number 30 in the local decision process, how would I bake that into the config? Trying to handle route selection quirks without messing up existing policies.
---
## Proposed answer:
To prioritize routes from BGP neighbor group "group_alpha" with a local preference of 30, use this configuration:

```
bgp
 group group_alpha
  local-preference 30
```

This sets the Local Preference attribute to 30 for all routes received from "group_alpha," making them more preferred in BGP path selection (higher values are preferred). The change applies only to this specific group without affecting other policies.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
peer <group-name> preferred-value <preferredvalue>
undo peer <group-name> preferred-value
FuncDef:
The peer preferred-value command sets a preferred value for the routes that a BGP device learns from its peer.
The undo peer preferred-value command deletes the preferred value set for the routes that a BGP device learns from its peer.
By default, the preferred value of a route learned from a BGP peer is 0.
ParaDef:
{'Parameters': 'group-name', 'Info': 'Specifies the name of a peer group.\nThe name is a string of 1 to 47 case-sensitive characters, with spaces not supported. When double quotation marks are used around the string, spaces are allowed in the string.'}
{'Parameters': 'preferredvalue', 'Info': 'Specifies the preferred value of the routes that a BGP device learns from its peer.\nThe value is an integer ranging from 0 to 65535.'}
