---
## User question:
Hey, on my Huawei NE40E box, I need to make sure that when it gets BGP route updates from the cluster of peers we named "europe_peers," it only lets through the ones that match the rules in our "transit_filter" setup. What steps should I take to get this working without breaking existing peering?
---
## Proposed answer:
To apply the "transit_filter" policy to BGP route updates from the "europe_peers" group, use the following configuration:

```cli
configure router bgp
  group "europe_peers"
    import "transit_filter"
  exit
exit
```

This applies the import policy to all peers in the "europe_peers" group, ensuring only routes matching "transit_filter" are accepted. Existing peering sessions will re-establish automatically with the new policy.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
peer <group-name> route-policy <route-policy-name> import
undo peer <group-name> route-policy <route-policy-name> import
FuncDef:
The peer route-policy import command specifies a route-policy for filtering routes received from a peer group.
The undo peer route-policy import command deletes a specified route-policy.
By default, no route-policy is configured for filtering routes received from a peer group.
ParaDef:
{'Parameters': 'group-name', 'Info': 'Specifies the name of a peer group.\nThe name is a string of 1 to 47 case-sensitive characters, with spaces not supported. When double quotation marks are used around the string, spaces are allowed in the string.'}
{'Parameters': 'route-policy-name', 'Info': 'Specifies the name of a route-policy.\nThe name is a string of 1 to 200 case-sensitive characters, with spaces not supported. When double quotation marks are used around the string, spaces are allowed in the string.'}
{'Parameters': 'import', 'Info': 'Applies a route-policy to routes to be imported from a peer group.\n-'}
---
