To prevent BGP from altering the next hop for routes advertised to neighbors on the same subnet, use the `next-hop-unchanged` command. Apply it to the relevant BGP group or neighbor configuration:

**Group-level configuration:**
```cli
configure
router bgp <AS-number>
  group <group-name>
    next-hop-unchanged
```

**Neighbor-level configuration:**
```cli
configure
router bgp <AS-number>
  group <group-name>
    neighbor <neighbor-ip-address>
      next-hop-unchanged
```

**Key notes:**
1. Replace `<AS-number>`, `<group-name>`, and `<neighbor-ip-address>` with your specific values
2. This command preserves the original next hop attribute when advertising routes to specified peers
3. Applies to both IPv4 and IPv6 routes by default (omit optional `label-ipv4`/`label-ipv6` parameters unless specifically needed for labeled routes)
4. Configure this for all BGP peers sharing the same LAN segment where direct reachability exists

The configuration prevents unnecessary next-hop modifications while maintaining proper routing between directly connected neighbors.
