## Proposed answer:
To prevent the router from altering the next-hop address for IPv6 routes advertised to directly connected neighbors on the same Ethernet segment, configure the BGP group or neighbor with the `next-hop-unchanged` command. Here are the CLI commands:

```cli
system-view
router bgp [your_AS_number]
group [group_name]
 next-hop-unchanged
```

Alternatively, for a specific neighbor:
```cli
system-view
router bgp [your_AS_number]
neighbor [neighbor_IPv6_address]
 next-hop-unchanged
```

Replace `[your_AS_number]`, `[group_name]`, and `[neighbor_IPv6_address]` with your specific BGP settings. This ensures the original next-hop address is preserved when advertising IPv6 routes.
