To ensure non-public AS identifiers are scrubbed from BGP routes received from the neighbor at 192.168.5.20, apply the following configuration:

```cli
configure router bgp
  group <your-group-name>
    neighbor 192.168.5.20
      remove-private
    exit
  exit
exit
```

Replace `<your-group-name>` with the appropriate BGP group containing the neighbor. This command removes private AS numbers from the AS path in inbound routes before processing.
------------
As a network device expert, please determine whether the following answer correctly responds to the user's question based on the router configuration manual. Only output "Correct" or "Incorrect" without any explanation or additional information. You must make your own judgment based on the Router configuration manual provided to you, not on your own knowledge.
---
## User question:
Hey, I’ve got a Huawei NE40E box that’s taking BGP routes from a neighbor at 192.168.5.20, but those updates keep sneaking in internal AS identifiers in their path history. How do I tweak the config to make sure those non-public ASNs get scrubbed out completely before processing the routes?
---
## Proposed answer:
To ensure non-public AS identifiers are scrubbed from BGP routes received from the neighbor at 192.168.5.20, apply the following configuration:

```cli
configure router bgp
  group <your-group-name>
    neighbor 192.168.5.20
      remove-private
    exit
  exit
exit
```

Replace `<your-group-name>` with the appropriate BGP group containing the neighbor. This command removes private AS numbers from the AS path in inbound routes before processing.
