---
## User question:
I’m setting up BGP for a VPNv4 route instance on a Huawei NE40E and need to adjust how long the router waits for a specific neighbor (192.168.5.8) to recover properly after a restart—like if things get interrupted. How do I cap that waiting period to 180 seconds so it doesn’t linger too long before moving on?
---
## Proposed answer:
To configure the router to wait no longer than 180 seconds for neighbor `192.168.5.8` to recover after a restart, use the following commands:

```
bgp <your_as_number>
 peer 192.168.5.8
  graceful-restart
   restart-time 180
   quit
  quit
 quit
```

Replace `<your_as_number>` with your BGP autonomous system number. This sets the graceful restart recovery timer specifically for `192.168.5.8` to 180 seconds.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
peer <ipv4-address> local-graceful-restart timer restart <restart-time>
undo peer <ipv4-address> local-graceful-restart timer restart
FuncDef:
The peer local-graceful-restart timer restart command sets the maximum duration for a device to wait for the BGP peer relationship with a specified peer to be reestablished. After this command is run, the device will not advertise the maximum duration to the specified peer.
The undo peer local-graceful-restart timer restart command deletes the configured duration.
By default, a device waits for the peer relationship with a peer to be reestablished for a maximum of 150 seconds.
ParaDef:
{'Parameters': 'ipv4-address', 'Info': 'Specifies the IPv4 address of a BGP peer.\nThe value is in dotted decimal notation.'}
{'Parameters': 'restart-time', 'Info': 'Specifies the maximum duration for a device to wait for the GR recovery of a specified peer.\nThe value is an integer ranging from 3 to 3600, in seconds.'}
