## Proposed answer:
To configure the BGP hold time to 500 seconds and adjust the keepalive timer for neighbor 192.168.1.15:

```cli
router bgp [AS]
 group [group-name]
  neighbor 192.168.1.15
   hold-time 500
   keepalive 166
```

**Explanation:**  
- **`hold-time 500`** sets the maximum wait period to 500 seconds before considering the neighbor down.  
- **`keepalive 166`** reduces keepalive frequency to 166 seconds (approximately one-third of the hold time), minimizing unnecessary traffic while maintaining session stability.  

**Important notes:**  
1. Replace `[AS]` with your local BGP AS number and `[group-name]` with the peer group containing this neighbor.  
2. The peer must also support a 500-second hold time; the session will use the **lowest** hold time proposed by either router.  
3. Ensure the peer's configuration allows slow convergence after restarts to prevent session flaps.  

These changes make the router more tolerant to delayed route updates from the neighbor during recovery events.
